# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored; this file isn't.

# Where the sandbox Mail tab sends a test. Your own inbox — never a customer's.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ because the sandbox displays it; it is a build tool, not a
# product screen, and the value is your own address.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CRAIG_TEST_INBOX=you@example.com

# Server-side only. No NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, ever — that would publish the key
# to every browser that loads the page.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# What Craig sends from. Leave it unset and mail goes out as
# onboarding@resend.dev, Resend's shared sandbox address: it needs no DNS and
# only delivers to the address that owns the Resend account.
#
# The real one is craig@mail.craig-ob.me, and Resend refuses it with a 403 until
# that subdomain has its records. Set this the hour they land and every send and
# every preview moves across without a deploy.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ because the preview renders it, and a preview showing a different
# address to the one that arrives is a preview that lies. It is in the header of
# every email anyway — there is nothing here to keep from a browser.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CRAIG_MAIL_FROM=craig@mail.craig-ob.me

# Who gets a blind copy of every invitation the showcase sends.
#
# Invitations are the one thing here addressed to somebody who isn't you, so
# this is how you read what a stranger actually received rather than trusting
# the green tick. Blind, and on the same send: a visible cc would put this
# address on a new starter's welcome email, and a second send is a second thing
# that can fail.
#
# Not NEXT_PUBLIC_ — it is a recipient, not a screen. The default below is also
# in the route, so an environment that forgets this still copies somebody; an
# invitation copy that silently stops arriving is worse than none.
SHOWCASE_INVITE_BCC=dzakyayrosihan@gmail.com

# The showcase account is no longer configured here. It gets created by signing
# up, hashed, and held in memory until the server restarts or the sandbox resets
# it — so SHOWCASE_EMAIL and SHOWCASE_PASSWORD are gone rather than demoted. A
# variable that looks like a credential and gates nothing is worse than no
# variable at all.

# Who may create an account, in production.
#
# An account is not an inert row: it can call the invite route straight away,
# which sends a real email from our verified domain, to any address, signed with
# the company name typed at sign-up. Open sign-up plus that endpoint is a relay
# for convincing phishing, and the bill for it is a suspended Resend account.
#
# So production is closed unless one of these says otherwise. Development is
# always open with both unset, so none of this is in the way locally.
#
# SHOWCASE_SIGN_UP=open is the deliberate "yes, anybody" — set it if this is a
# public showcase and you want strangers to be able to try it.
# SHOWCASE_SIGN_UP=open
#
# SHOWCASE_SIGN_UP_ALLOW is a comma-separated list of whole addresses and
# @domain suffixes. When set it wins outright, in every environment, and
# SHOWCASE_SIGN_UP cannot widen it.
# SHOWCASE_SIGN_UP_ALLOW=you@example.com,@yourcompany.com

# Signs Craig's own tokens: the new starter's magic link and the Google OAuth
# state cookie. (The admin's session moved to Supabase Auth and no longer uses
# this.) Anyone who has it can mint a valid joiner link, so it is a secret in
# the strong sense — never NEXT_PUBLIC_, never committed, and different per
# environment. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Changing it invalidates every outstanding invitation link.
SESSION_SECRET=

# --- Supabase -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The database and the admin's sign-in. The project URL and publishable key are
# not secrets — the publishable key can read nothing, because every table has
# row-level security enabled with no policies. They are still server-side here
# because no browser talks to Supabase directly; everything goes through our
# routes.
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# The server's credential, and the only key that can touch the data. From the
# dashboard: project → Settings → API Keys → sb_secret_…. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_,
# never committed — anyone holding it holds every row in the database.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# --- The new starter's personal details ---------------------------------------
#
# What the "Personal details" block's answers are encrypted with before they
# are written to the database — AES-256-GCM, 32 bytes, as 64 hex characters or
# base64 that decodes to 32. The database never sees the plaintext. Generate one
# with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# That block collects a legal name, a date of birth, a home address and a mobile
# number in one answer, which together are what somebody opens a credit account
# with. A refresh token next door gets this treatment because it is standing
# permission that can at least be revoked; a date of birth is a fact about a
# person for the rest of their life, so it gets the same envelope and its own
# key. Deliberately not GOOGLE_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY, and deliberately not
# SESSION_SECRET: one key, one job. Rotating a provider's key breaks connections
# somebody can re-make by pressing Connect; rotating this one destroys answers
# that exist only because a stranger typed them once, and they would have to be
# asked for all over again.
#
# With this unset, nothing is collected — the form is withheld from the new
# starter and the route refuses. A deployment that quietly fell back to
# plaintext the day somebody forgot a variable would be worse than one with no
# encryption at all, because everything around it would still say "encrypted".
# JOINER_PII_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- Google Workspace ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# A customer connects their own Google Workspace once, as a super admin, and
# what comes back is a refresh token: standing permission for this deployment
# to create accounts inside their company, months later, with nobody present.
# That is what makes the product work and it is also the most dangerous thing
# stored here — worse than a password, because no password manager will ever
# prompt anybody to rotate it.
#
# All four are server-side only. None of them may ever gain a NEXT_PUBLIC_
# prefix, and two of them could not survive one.
#
# Our application's identity with Google, from a Web application OAuth client
# in the Google Cloud console. One set, shared by every customer — these are
# never a customer's credentials. The Admin SDK API has to be enabled in the
# same project, and admin.directory.user listed on the consent screen.
#
# The redirect URI is configuration rather than something derived from the
# request: Google compares it byte for byte against the Authorized redirect
# URIs on the client, and the alternative — building it from the incoming Host
# header — lets a crafted header steer where an authorisation code is sent. It
# must end in /api/google/callback.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=xxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/google/callback
#
# What the refresh token is encrypted with before it is written to the
# database — AES-256-GCM, 32 bytes, as 64 hex characters or base64 that
# decodes to 32. The database never sees the plaintext. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# Deliberately not SESSION_SECRET. Rotating the session secret is a routine act
# that signs everyone out for a minute; if it were also this, it would silently
# destroy every Google connection at the same time, and nobody would find out
# until a new starter didn't get an account.
#
# With this unset, connecting is refused outright rather than the token being
# written down in the clear — and the sandbox's Google tab says so instead of
# offering a button that can only fail.
# GOOGLE_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- Slack --------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A customer connects their Slack workspace once, as a workspace admin, and
# what comes back is a bot token: standing permission, with no expiry, to read
# the workspace's directory and manage its public channels. No workspace has
# ever been connected — the flow is unverified end to end until one is.
#
# What this deliberately cannot do: invite somebody to the workspace itself.
# Slack reserves that (admin.users.invite) for Enterprise Grid organisations,
# so on a normal workspace the invite stays with a human admin and the scopes
# below cover what happens after the person joins.
#
# Our app's identity with Slack, registered once at api.slack.com. The
# redirect URI must be https (Slack allows no localhost exception — local
# development needs a tunnel), must end in /api/slack/callback, and must match
# a Redirect URL on the app config character for character. In production it
# is the stable alias, never a per-build URL:
#   https://craig-alpha.vercel.app/api/slack/callback
# SLACK_CLIENT_ID=
# SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET=
# SLACK_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=https://craig-alpha.vercel.app/api/slack/callback
#
# What the bot token is encrypted with before it is written to the database —
# same contract as GOOGLE_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY, and deliberately a different
# variable: one key, one job, so rotating one provider's key cannot silently
# destroy the other's connections. 32 bytes, hex or base64:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# With this unset, connecting is refused outright rather than the token being
# written down in the clear.
# SLACK_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# --- Linear -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The same model as Google Workspace, pointed at Linear: a workspace admin
# connects once, and what comes back is a refresh token this deployment keeps.
# Unverified end to end — no Linear OAuth application has been registered and
# no workspace has ever been connected. Nothing runs on the connection yet;
# it exists so the "invite to Linear" block can be honest about needing it.
#
# One difference from Google worth knowing before debugging anything: since
# April 2026 Linear rotates tokens. The access token dies after 24 hours and
# every refresh replaces the refresh token too, so whatever runner eventually
# uses this must write the rotated token back or the connection dies within a
# day of first use.
#
# Our application's identity with Linear, from an OAuth application created in
# the workspace's API settings (linear.app/settings/api/applications). One
# set, shared by every customer — never a customer's credentials. All three
# server-side only; the redirect URI is configuration for exactly Google's
# reasons, and must end in /api/linear/callback. In production it is
# https://craig-alpha.vercel.app/api/linear/callback — the stable alias, never
# a per-build URL.
# LINEAR_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# LINEAR_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# LINEAR_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/linear/callback
#
# What the Linear refresh token is encrypted with before it is written to the
# database. Same shape and same generator as the Google key, and deliberately
# a separate variable — one key, one job: rotating either provider's key must
# not orphan the other provider's connections.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# LINEAR_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- Google directory notifications -------------------------------------------
#
# Where Google POSTs when a customer's directory changes, which is how a new
# starter's Workspace step settles itself the moment they first sign in rather
# than the next time somebody opens their page.
#
# Normally unset. The address is derived from GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI's
# origin plus /api/google/notifications — the redirect URI is already the one
# place this deployment states its own public origin, and a second variable
# naming the same host is a second variable to forget on a deploy. In production
# that derivation gives https://craig-alpha.vercel.app/api/google/notifications,
# which is what Google is told.
#
# Set it only when the derivation cannot work, which is exactly one case:
# development. GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI is http://localhost:3000/... locally,
# and Google accepts a loopback address for OAuth and refuses it outright for a
# webhook — so this is where a public tunnel goes if you need to exercise
# notifications locally. Left unset in development, no channel is created,
# nothing breaks, and acceptance is picked up by the existing check on a page
# view exactly as it was before.
#
# It must be public https with a certificate Google trusts, and the domain has
# to be one registered for the Cloud project. Google refuses an address it will
# not deliver to when the channel is created, not at delivery time.
# GOOGLE_WATCH_ADDRESS=

# Who may run the channel renewal sweep at /api/google/watch/renew.
#
# A push channel expires — Gmail documents seven days for users.watch and the
# Admin SDK does not commit to a number — and when it lapses, nothing errors:
# Google simply stops delivering and every Workspace step goes back to settling
# only when somebody looks. So the sweep has to run on a timer, and the timer is
# a scheduler with no account, which is why this is a shared secret rather than
# a session. Vercel Cron sends it as `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`.
#
# With this unset the sweep refuses every caller rather than running open. An
# unauthenticated version of that endpoint is a public URL that makes one Google
# API call per connected customer on demand, which is a way of exhausting
# somebody else's quota from the outside.
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# NOTHING SCHEDULES THIS YET. There is no crons entry in vercel.json — Vercel
# rejects a schedule the account's plan doesn't allow at deploy time, and a
# failed deploy is a worse outcome than a sweep somebody adds on purpose. Until
# it is scheduled, channels are created but never renewed and push stops working
# after the first expiry.
# CRON_SECRET=
